window.docContents[250].resolve({"/1___-Introduction/Chapter-1___-Introduction/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--1___-Introduction--Chapter-1___-Introduction":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00091. Introduction","header":"Chapter 1: Introduction","id":"/1___-Introduction/Chapter-1___-Introduction/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--1___-Introduction--Chapter-1___-Introduction"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___1___-What-is-representation-theory___/Dynkin-diagrams-significance_SEMI_-representation-theory-of-finite-groups-overview/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___1___-What-is-representation-theory___--Dynkin-diagrams-significance_SEMI_-representation-theory-of-finite-groups-overview--Dynkin-diagrams-significance_SEMI_-representation-theory-of-finite-groups-overview--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"Declaration: IsSemisimpleRing.isSemisimpleModule\n\nAlignment metadata: book-ref=Chapter2/Discussion_after_Theorem2.1.2/Derived2; role=supporting\n\nDeclaration: MonoidAlgebra\n\nAlignment metadata: book-ref=Chapter2/Discussion_after_Theorem2.1.2; role=supporting\n\nDeclaration: MonoidAlgebra.single_mul_single\n\nAlignment metadata: book-ref=Chapter2/Discussion_after_Theorem2.1.2; role=supporting\n\nA semisimple module satisfying the auxiliary predicate is simple.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Discussion_after_Theorem2.1.2; role=supporting]\n\nThe group algebra of a finite group over a field is semisimple when the group order is a unit in the field.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Discussion_after_Theorem2.1.2/Derived2; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter4/Discussion_after_Theorem4.6.3; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter4/Theorem4.1.1; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter4/Theorem4.2.1/Derived2; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.1. What is representation theory?\u0009Dynkin diagrams significance; representation theory of finite groups overview\u0009Dynkin diagrams significance; representation theory of finite groups overview\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___1___-What-is-representation-theory___/Dynkin-diagrams-significance_SEMI_-representation-theory-of-finite-groups-overview/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___1___-What-is-representation-theory___--Dynkin-diagrams-significance_SEMI_-representation-theory-of-finite-groups-overview--Dynkin-diagrams-significance_SEMI_-representation-theory-of-finite-groups-overview--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___3___-Representations/Irreducible-_LPAR_simple_RPAR_-representation/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___3___-Representations--Irreducible-_LPAR_simple_RPAR_-representation--Irreducible-_LPAR_simple_RPAR_-representation--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"An auxiliary predicate on a module over a ring.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Definition2.3.5; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Discussion_2.1_irreducible_indecomposable; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.3. Representations\u0009Irreducible (simple) representation\u0009Irreducible (simple) representation\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___3___-Representations/Irreducible-_LPAR_simple_RPAR_-representation/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___3___-Representations--Irreducible-_LPAR_simple_RPAR_-representation--Irreducible-_LPAR_simple_RPAR_-representation--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___3___-Representations/Proof-of-Corollary-2___3___10/#Chapter2___Discussion_proof_Corollary2___3___10___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.3. Representations\u0009Proof of Corollary 2.3.10\u0009Proof of Corollary 2.3.10","header":"Formalization","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___3___-Representations/Proof-of-Corollary-2___3___10/#Chapter2___Discussion_proof_Corollary2___3___10___formalization"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___9___-Lie-algebras/Concrete-examples-of-Lie-algebras___-R___3___-sl_LPAR_n_RPAR____-Heisenberg/#Chapter2___Discussion_concrete_Lie_examples":{"contents":"Here are a few more concrete examples of Lie algebras:\n\n(1) \\mathbb{R}^3 with [u, v] = u \\times v, the cross-product of u and v.\n\n(2) \\mathfrak{sl}(n), the set of n \\times n matrices with trace 0. For example, \\mathfrak{sl}(2) has the basis\n\ne = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\end{pmatrix}, \\qquad f = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\end{pmatrix}, \\qquad h = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\end{pmatrix}\n\nwith relations\n\n[h, e] = 2e, \\quad [h, f] = -2f, \\quad [e, f] = h.\n\n(3) The Heisenberg Lie algebra \\mathcal{H} of matrices \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & * & * \\\\ 0 & 0 & * \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\end{pmatrix}. It has the basis\n\nx = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\end{pmatrix}, \\qquad y = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\end{pmatrix}, \\qquad c = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\end{pmatrix}\n\nwith relations [y, x] = c and [y, c] = [x, c] = 0.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.9. Lie algebras\u0009Concrete examples of Lie algebras: R^3, sl(n), Heisenberg","header":"Concrete examples of Lie algebras: R^3, sl(n), Heisenberg","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___9___-Lie-algebras/Concrete-examples-of-Lie-algebras___-R___3___-sl_LPAR_n_RPAR____-Heisenberg/#Chapter2___Discussion_concrete_Lie_examples"},"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___4___-Filtrations/Filtration-of-a-representation/#Chapter3___Definition3___4___1___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00093. General results of representation theory\u00093.4. Filtrations\u0009Filtration of a representation\u0009Filtration of a representation","header":"Formalization","id":"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___4___-Filtrations/Filtration-of-a-representation/#Chapter3___Definition3___4___1___formalization"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___24___-Problems/V____lambda-___-V_lambda-and-V_lambda-___-C_--___-V____lambda______/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___24___-Problems--V____lambda-___-V_lambda-and-V_lambda-___-C_--___-V____lambda______--V____lambda-___-V_lambda-and-V_lambda-___-C_--___-V____lambda______--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"For every partition, there is a function compatible with the action of each finite\npermutation.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Problem5.24.1; role=supporting]\n\nAssociates a submodule of the ambient module to each partition of a natural number.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Problem5.24.1; role=supporting]\n\nA self-map of the partitions of a natural number.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Problem5.24.1; role=supporting]\n\nThere exists a map from the indicated partition-indexed subtype that intertwines permutation action with its sign twist.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Problem5.24.1; role=supporting]\n\nThe complex-algebra endomorphism that twists permutation monomials by their signs.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Problem5.24.1; role=supporting]\n\nOn a permutation monomial, the sign twist multiplies that monomial by the sign of the permutation.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Problem5.24.1; role=supporting]\n\nThe action of a sign-twisted permutation monomial is its original action scaled by the permutation sign.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Problem5.24.1; role=supporting]\n\nThe sign-twisting algebra endomorphism is bijective.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Problem5.24.1; role=supporting]\n\nThe image under the sign twist of the ideal generated by one element is the ideal generated by its image.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Problem5.24.1; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.24. Problems\u0009V'_lambda ≅ V_lambda and V_lambda ⊗ C_- = V_{lambda*}\u0009V'_lambda ≅ V_lambda and V_lambda ⊗ C_- = V_{lambda*}\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___24___-Problems/V____lambda-___-V_lambda-and-V_lambda-___-C_--___-V____lambda______/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___24___-Problems--V____lambda-___-V_lambda-and-V_lambda-___-C_--___-V____lambda______--V____lambda-___-V_lambda-and-V_lambda-___-C_--___-V____lambda______--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___6___-Adjoint-functors/Uniqueness-of-adjoints-and-analogy-with-linear-algebra/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--7___-Introduction-to-categories--7___6___-Adjoint-functors--Uniqueness-of-adjoints-and-analogy-with-linear-algebra--Uniqueness-of-adjoints-and-analogy-with-linear-algebra--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"Declaration: CategoryTheory.Adjunction.leftAdjointUniq\n\nAlignment metadata: book-ref=Chapter7/Discussion_after_Definition7.6.1; role=supporting\n\nDeclaration: CategoryTheory.Adjunction.rightAdjointUniq\n\nAlignment metadata: book-ref=Chapter7/Discussion_after_Definition7.6.1; role=supporting\n\nThe composite of a right adjoint with the covariant hom functor at an object is corepresented by the image of that object under the left adjoint.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter7/Discussion_after_Definition7.6.1; role=supporting]\n\nThe composite of the opposite left adjoint with the contravariant hom functor at an object is represented by the image of that object under the right adjoint.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter7/Discussion_after_Definition7.6.1; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00097. Introduction to categories\u00097.6. Adjoint functors\u0009Uniqueness of adjoints and analogy with linear algebra\u0009Uniqueness of adjoints and analogy with linear algebra\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___6___-Adjoint-functors/Uniqueness-of-adjoints-and-analogy-with-linear-algebra/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--7___-Introduction-to-categories--7___6___-Adjoint-functors--Uniqueness-of-adjoints-and-analogy-with-linear-algebra--Uniqueness-of-adjoints-and-analogy-with-linear-algebra--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"}});